<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n68335" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μόλῐς</span></b> , Adv., post-<i><span class="en">
			Hom.
		</span></i> synonym of <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μόγις</span>, prevailing in
		Trag., Com., and 
			Att.
		 prose, though in <i><span class="en">
			Pl.
		</span></i> and later prose <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μόγις</span> was
		preferred (in <i><span class="en">
			A.
		</span></i> the Laur. Ms. gives each form twice, the same Ms. of <i><span class="en">
			S.
		</span></i>
		<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μόλις</span> always); <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μ. μέν, ἀλλ᾽ ὅμως ἠνεσχόμην</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Ar.
					<u>Nu.</u>
					1363
				</span></i>
			, cf. <i><span class="en">
				S.
				<u>Ant.</u>
				290
			</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
				1105
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>El.</u>
				575
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Ph.</u>
				329
			</span></i>; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ζῶντι καὶ μάλα μ.</span> nay,
				<i>only just</i> alive, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Tht.</u>
				142b
			</span></i> (cod. W <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μόγις</span>)<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">; μ. καὶ ἠρέμα πάσχειν</span>
			<i>scarcely at all</i> . . , <i><span class="en">
				Arist.
				<u>Metaph.</u>
				1019a31
			</span></i> (cod. Ab <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μόγις</span>)
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">; ἢ ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν ἢ μ.</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Id.
					<u>Ph.</u>
					217b32
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μ. πάνυ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Eub.
					30
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">πάνυ μ.</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Philem.
					88.8
				</span></i>
			: with a neg., <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οὐ μ.</span>
			<i>not scarcely</i>, i.e. <i>quite, utterly</i>, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀπώλεσας οὐ μ.</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					A.
					<u>Ag.</u>
					1082
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">θυραῖος ἔστω πόλεμος, οὐ μ. παρών</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Id.
					<u>Eu.</u>
					864
				</span></i>
			 (where Sch. explains <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οὐ μ.</span>
			by <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οὐ μακράν</span>, but the sense is
			dub.); 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">θέλουσαν οὐ μ. καλεῖς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					E.
					<u>Hel.</u>
					334
				</span></i>
			 (lyr.).</div>
